# The Knotted Tails — A Supplemental Storyline for Mask of the Oni

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game — The Knotted Tails (a supplemental storyline for Mask of the Oni)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games (2020)

> GM-facing narrative. Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim; intervening GM procedure is
> summarized faithfully — the full detail lives in the source PDF. The Dramatis Personae statblocks
> are in `l5r5e-0.4-knotted-tails-cast.ttrpg` and the part structure in `l5r5e-0.4-knotted-tails.arc`.
> This is an optional bonus storyline run within *Mask of the Oni* (before Shiro Hiruma) or any
> Shadowlands campaign; it uses the players' existing characters, so there are no pregens.

---

## Part 1: Storyline Background

The Knotted Tails, a nezumi tribe that prides itself on remembering the past, believe their ancestors were once close allies of the Hiruma family. Some of these ancestors are now **Transcendents**. Whether real or fabricated, this history colors the tribe's habits and traditions. Though they move their village periodically, as many tribes do, their shrinking territory still includes **Shiro Hiruma**, and they remain protective of the ruins. They are too fearful to venture inside, however, and warn travelers of dangers like ghosts, goblins, and other evil beings — a fear that can add to the growing tension of *Mask of the Oni*.

The nezumi are human-sized, rat-like sentient denizens of the Shadowlands who are free of Fu Leng's influence. The Knotted Tails' leaders follow the way of the samurai as much as they know how; the tribe considers itself honorable and keeps honor scores reflecting this loyalty to the samurai code, and higher-than-typical glory because the tribe often helps lost Crab scouts. Nezumi names are fluid and change with major life events; the Dreamer One Eye was once named Twitching Tail. The threat: something has been stealing the tribe's scouts and pups in the night, and the PCs can find and slay it — making useful allies — or, if they treat the nezumi as just another foe, add to the tribe's problems.

---

## Part 2: Being Watched

As the PCs move through the Shadowlands near Shiro Hiruma, a patrol of Knotted Tails warriors follows them:

> As you walk, the air grows still and close. The only sound is that of your own hollow footfalls, the only movement the dust each step raises from the bone-dry ground... Then, something suddenly skitters across the ground. You catch sight of something large and sinuous disappearing into a large fissure and hear the scrape of claws.

A TN 2 Survival (Water) check spots the rat-like watchers; a TN 3 Culture (Earth) check identifies them as nezumi (a Crab PC knows automatically). Confronted, the huddled, frightened nezumi explain they are the Knotted Tails on patrol; the scout **Nine Claws** requests the PCs come to their village, claiming their Dreamer foresaw samurai who would help. Cooperating costs glory (equal to the PCs' glory rank) if it were discovered; a Crab Escort from *Mask of the Oni* will vouch that scouts sometimes accept nezumi help. The nezumi warn of the castle ("Once you get in, you can't get out!") and of the unknown threat.

### The Patrol & The Parasite

The scouts share what they can (a TN 3 Culture check gauges their honesty). If the injured Crab **Hida Kurumi** (from *Mask of the Oni*) is with the PCs, the nezumi offer to expel his parasite with a special brew from the Dreamer:

> The nezumi surround Kurumi, blocking your view, but their continued squeaking cannot mask his moans and cries as the potent concoction does its work. As he begins to retch, the nezumi suddenly scatter, just as a dark worm-like shape lurches from Kurumi's mouth, skids across the ground in a squirming, slimy mass, and slithers quickly towards the closest viable host.

---

## Part 3: The Knotted Tails Village

Travelling with the scouts, the PCs reach the village:

> The nezumi pause as they reach the brow of a hill, looking expectantly at you. Standing on the crest, you get your first view of the nezumi village, a scattering of mismatched tents hidden amongst the rocks below. Furred figures emerge from numerous burrows and stand to watch your approach. The smallest among them, nezumi about the size of human infants, swarm up the hill on all fours, squeaking excitedly.

Playful **pups** greet the party (harming them turns the whole tribe hostile). Much of the village is underground.

### The Leader's Tent

At the center stands the leader's tent, its faded Crab banner and a knotted-tail emblem on the flaps:

> This tent is larger than the others, made from skins and furs stretched over a wooden frame... A large grey-furred nezumi with long, drooping whiskers sits cross-legged beside the light, and she bows her head low in greeting. She is dressed in ill-fitting Crab armor, dented but polished to a shine. A horned helmet and wakizashi rest on a makeshift stand behind her... "Greetings, honored samurai, I am Lucky Silver. Our Dreamer told me of your coming. I believe we can help each other."

**Lucky Silver** believes her tribe's ancestors were close allies of the Hiruma and considers herself an honorary samurai — a position she believes inherited from her predecessor **Silver Ears**, whose Kaiu wakizashi she bears. She warns of the **Unknown Threat**: victims vanish from the village's edges, and even sleepers wander off into the night, never seen again. Her suspects: a "blood painter" (a mahō-tsukai — which may tie into the pursuit of Kitsu Sokori from *Mask of the Oni*), a Kuni shugenja seeking test subjects ("painted samurai"), or an aggressive rival tribe.

### The Rememberer, the Shrine to Memory, and the Dreamer

The tribe's **Rememberer** — a frail, aged nezumi — keeps the tribe's oral history and tells the tale of Silver Ears and the Hiruma. The **Shrine to Memory** holds relics of the tribe's past. The Dreamer **One Eye** (once Twitching Tail), revered above even the leader, spends his time dreamdwelling and communing with Transcendents in the Realm of Dreams; distracted and cryptic, he urges the PCs to "save the shadow" — **Quick Shadow**, the Rememberer's apprentice, sent to the graveyard and now in danger. What becomes clear is that the threat is a nezumi angry at the tribe, especially Lucky Silver — someone who remembers the village and knows the tribe.

---

## Part 4: The Nezumi Graveyard

The nezumi graveyard is a natural pit filled over centuries with the exposed bones of the tribes' dead — a horrifying sight to a samurai, but to the nezumi a sign of respect, for their dead are not hidden and Rememberers can recognize their skulls. Searching for Quick Shadow:

> Large posts have been stuck into the ground surrounding the pit, marked by scraps of cloth and bits of ribbon. Tufts of fur snagged on the jagged rocks near the edge of the pit flutter, caught by a wind you cannot feel. Drops of dark blood staining the ground suggest a recent struggle. Following them away from the pit, you find the ripped and bloodied bodies of two Knotted Tails scouts. A little further beyond the bodies you see the patched robes of a Rememberer — but she is nowhere to be found.

### The Confrontation

The ambush springs: entranced Knotted Tails and abducted or exiled **outcast nezumi**, all under the control of **Frayed Whisker** — a nezumi Lucky Silver banished years ago, now older than he looks and tormented by waking and sleeping visions. From a distance he controls and curses the nezumi (see his **Dreamwalker** ability), calling out his grievance:

> See me, Lucky Silver? See Frayed Whisker, stronger now! You are weak, weak like the Dreamer and weak like the tribe. Abandoned me, abandoned the one who could fight back the bad that comes! Die now, die! You are the bad now!

Lucky Silver, shocked he has not aged a day, tries to reach him: *"You…you have not aged one day! Frayed Whisker, please, you do not need to do this anymore. The Dreamer believes he can help!"* If One Eye is present, his Dreamreader ability frees the controlled nezumi one by one. If Frayed Whisker is killed or knocked unconscious, those he dreamwalks are released. If captured alive, One Eye or a powerful shugenja might complete his training or sever his tie to the Realm of Dreams to save him. Terrain in the graveyard is Defiled and Dangerous; the great pit is Dangerous, and entering it for any reason inflicts strife (and costs honor and glory if entered willingly).

**Leaving Lucky Silver to her fate:** if the PCs abandon her, they forfeit 3 honor for violating the Bushidō tenets of Jin (Compassion) and Yū (Courage); alone against Frayed Whisker's horde she dies, and the leaderless village is later decimated.

---

## Part 5: Resolution

If Frayed Whisker dies during the confrontation, the outcast nezumi scatter and the Knotted Tails are safe. The grateful tribe offers gifts (such as an item from the Shrine to Memory) and, if Lucky Silver survives, pledges loyalty — giving the PCs the **Support of the Knotted Tails** advantage. They also offer the infiltrator **Three Tooth** as a guide into Shiro Hiruma, letting the PCs navigate its tunnels with relative ease (though no Knotted Tails nezumi will explore the castle itself).

**Rewards:** 4 honor if the PCs risked their lives to save Lucky Silver (the Bushidō tenet of Yū, courage); 1 XP for completing the skirmish; 2 XP for freeing some or all of the controlled nezumi; 1 XP and 3 honor for capturing Frayed Whisker alive.

---

## Ties Back to Mask of the Oni

Run within *Mask of the Oni*, this storyline shares that adventure's Shadowlands setting. A **Crab Escort** (or named Crab such as **Hida Kurumi** or the loyal bushi **Hida Nagahide**) may accompany the PCs; the nezumi can treat Kurumi's parasite. The tribe's suspicion of a "blood painter" can steer the PCs toward the blood-sorcerer **Kitsu Sokori** of that adventure. After the graveyard, the PCs may proceed to Shiro Hiruma — with or without nezumi help — and if the Dreamer lives, they may meet a **Transcendent** in their dreams within the castle who offers cryptic aid.
